<p>The United Arab Emirates denied reports that Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu secretly visited the country during the war with Iran, after Netanyahu’s office said he met UAE President Sheikh Mohamed bin Zayed Al Nahyan during “Operation Lion’s Roar” and that the meeting produced a “historic breakthrough” in bilateral ties. The UAE Foreign Ministry denied reports about an alleged Netanyahu visit or that it had received any Israeli military delegation, adding that UAE relations with Israel are public, conducted under the Abraham Accords, and “not based on non-transparent or unofficial arrangements”. The denial followed remarks by U.S. Ambassador to Israel Mike Huckabee about Israeli air-defense assistance to the UAE during the war with Iran. Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi said Netanyahu had “publicly revealed” what Iranian security services had previously told Tehran’s leadership and warned that those working with Israel to sow division would be held to account. Several reports said the war followed U.S.-Israeli strikes on Iran at the end of February and that a ceasefire took effect last month.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/7sTJ5d7LAQ_tTX0NfJfy0v7cLPJhRBKeod5sRi7jI9N5UIHFl00izZ7rIrcYWacjYAqmJrZxUB2fWcCj5P7lz4utMnti2o6YaayxOOAdV0l3' alt='Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu delivers a statement at the Palmachim Air Force Base near the city of Rishon Lezion, Israel on July 5.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>No date: Netanyahu’s office did not disclose the exact date of the alleged visit, while CBS News reported, citing sources, that it took place in late March.</li><li>Visit claim: A source close to the alleged meeting told a Spanish-language outlet that Netanyahu and Sheikh Mohamed met in Al Ain on March 26 for several hours.</li><li>Rare rebuttal: The Financial Times characterized the UAE’s denial as an “extraordinary rebuttal” from Israel’s closest Arab partner.</li><li>Direct contacts: WAM previously reported that Netanyahu was among leaders who called Sheikh Mohamed to condemn Iranian attacks and express solidarity with the UAE.</li></ul><blockquote>Enmity with the Great People of Iran is a foolish gamble. Collusion with Israel in doing so: unforgivable. - Abbas Araghchi</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Israeli prime minister’s office: Dawn reported that the office stopped short of confirming Huckabee’s comments about the Iron Dome deployment when it announced the alleged visit. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/2000091/uae-denies-reports-of-secret-netanyahu-visit'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>UAE Foreign Ministry: The ministry said only UAE authorities could validate such claims and described unannounced visits or undisclosed arrangements as “entirely unfounded” unless officially announced. (<a href='https://www.thenationalnews.com/news/2026/05/13/uae-denies-reports-israeli-leader-benjamin-netanyahu-paid-visit/'>The National</a>)</li><li>Ziv Agmon: Netanyahu’s former chief of staff, who resigned in March, posted that he joined Netanyahu on the trip and that the prime minister was received with “the honor of kings.” (<a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/05/14/2026/uae-denies-israeli-pms-claim-of-historic-visit'>Semafor</a>)</li><li>Israeli officials: Israeli officials told Ynetnews that Abu Dhabi was upset by the disclosure but that they did not expect the dispute to derail the strategic alliance. (<a href='https://www.ynetnews.com/article/byqvgrmjze'>Ynetnews</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='http://www.euronews.com/2026/05/14/uae-denies-netanyahu-offices-claim-of-secret-wartime-visit-as-iran-dubs-it-unforgivable'>UAE denies Netanyahu office&#x27;s claim of secret wartime visit as Iran dubs it &#x27;unforgivable&#x27;</a> - euronews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/may/13/benjamin-netanyahu-made-secret-trip-to-uae-at-height-of-the-iran-war'>Benjamin Netanyahu says he made secret trip to UAE at height of Iran war</a> - theguardian.com</li><li